Accept zero-length AVPs in cdp, answer fixed-size ones of the wrong length.

RFC 6733 4.2: "The Data field is zero or more octets", and an empty
Grouped AVP is legal (TS 29.229 6.3.4). cdp's decoder built every received
AVP through AAACreateAVP(), which refuses length 0, so the whole message was
dropped: a UAA with an empty Server-Capabilities never reached ims_icscf, the
UAR timed out and the registration failed. Inside a group the same refusal
made AAAUngroupAVPS() return the members decoded so far, i.e. a group with
members silently missing. Both decoders also required 12 header bytes for
every AVP, so an empty AVP without the V bit (8 bytes) at the end of a
message or group was dropped as truncated.

Decode through a new AAACreateReceivedAVP(), which allows length 0 and
points empty AVPs at a static zero buffer, so readers that skip the length
check of a fixed-size type read zeros instead of the next bytes.
AAACreateAVP() is unchanged: callers that pass an empty string still get no
AVP. AAACloneAVP() clones empty AVPs. A malformed group now ungroups to an
empty list, as documented, instead of a truncated one. The minimum header
is 8 bytes, 12 with the V bit.

Fixed-size base protocol AVPs (Result-Code, Vendor-Id, Origin-State-Id,
also inside Vendor-Specific-Application-Id and Experimental-Result) must
keep their exact length. A request with a wrong one is answered with 5014
DIAMETER_INVALID_AVP_LENGTH (RFC 6733 7.1.5) and Failed-AVP holding the
offending AVP, wrapped in its group when nested (7.5), instead of being
dropped. Such an answer, or a request on a peer that is not open, is
dropped and logged.

